 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 5,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "THE CAIRNGORMS (Am Monadh Ruadh). Britain's largest national park, with five of the six highest mountains in the country. The plateau is the closest thing Britain has to the Arctic. Wrap up."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE REINDEER CENTRE. Britain's only free-ranging reindeer, brought here in 1952 by Mikel Utsi, a Sami herder. The males drop their antlers before Christmas, so Santa's team, still antlered, must be female."
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "CAIRN GORM. Gaelic for 'blue cairn' or 'blue mountain', a granite plateau up to 1,245 m. The cave in the crags is closed to visitors without a lamp. Seriously. Take a lamp. The sign says so, and the sign has never been wrong."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "THE OLD CALEDONIAN PINEWOOD. A scrap of the great forest that once covered Scotland. Capercaillie, crested tits and red squirrels live here. The male capercaillie dances in spring. Very proud, very silly."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "PEAT BOG. The black pools are deep and sticky. Don't step in, or you'll need a rescue and a new pair of boots. If you have a way to walk on bog, there's a dry hollow to the south-east with a view."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "cairngorms_ospreys",
   "title": "Ospreys",
   "text": "Ospreys had stopped breeding in Britain by 1916. In 1954 a pair quietly returned to nest in Strathspey, and at nearby Loch Garten volunteers guarded the nests day and night. Scotland now has hundreds of pairs. They catch fish feet first, which is braver than it looks."
  },
  {
   "x": 9,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "cairngorms_big_grey_man",
   "title": "The Big Grey Man",
   "text": "Am Fear Liath Mòr, the Big Grey Man, is said to haunt the summit of Ben Macdui. In 1925 the respected climber Professor Norman Collie said he had once heard giant footsteps crunching behind him in the mist, and fled. Scientists blame mist, shadows and tired legs. The footsteps have not commented."
  },
  {
   "x": 18,
   "y": 25,
   "plaque": "arts_nan_shepherd",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Nan Shepherd",
   "text": "Nan Shepherd, born near Aberdeen in 1893, walked these mountains all her life and wrote The Living Mountain about them in the 1940s: not a book about reaching summits but about being in a place, with your feet, your skin, the water and the weather. No publisher wanted it. It came out in 1977, four years before she died, and is now read as one of the great books about walking anywhere. Since 2016 her face has been on the Royal Bank of Scotland five-pound note. She would have spent it on a bus to the hills."
  }
 ],
